/*!

\page cartesiangridplugin_hypo_page Cartesian Grid Parameters hypothesis

The <b>Cartesian Grid 3D</b> algorithm needs one <b>Cartesian Grid Parameters</b> hypothesis. In the <b>Create
Mesh</b> dialog, choose the algorithm on the 3D tab, then create the hypothesis with the button next to it. Both must be
assigned to the main shape (the whole geometry).

The hypothesis says where the grid lines are. The dialog has one tab per axis (X, Y and Z), and each axis is set
independently: you can use a different mode on each axis.

<h2>The extent of the grid</h2>

By default the grid covers the <b>bounding box of the solids</b>: the smallest box, aligned with the axes, that contains
all the solids of the geometry. Only the solids count: construction points, edges or faces that are not part of a solid
do not make the grid bigger. In the modes below, "the extent" of an axis means the length of this bounding box along
that axis.

<h2>The four modes of an axis</h2>

- <b>Nb cells</b>: you give the number of cells n. The extent is divided into n equal cells.
- <b>Step</b>: you give the size s you would like for a cell. The plug-in computes the number of cells as the extent
  divided by s, rounded to the nearest whole number (at least 1), and then adjusts the cell size slightly so that the
  cells cover the bounding box exactly. The real cell size can therefore be a little different from s.
- <b>Coordinates</b>: you give the exact positions of the grid lines along the axis, as a sorted list of at least 2
  different values. This lets you use cells of different sizes. Here the grid goes from the first value to the last one,
  whatever the size of the bounding box: if a solid sticks out beyond these values, that part of the solid is not
  meshed, and a warning tells you so.

  In the dialog:
  - the list starts with the two bounds of the shape along that axis;
  - \b Insert adds a value after the selected one (the selected value plus the "insert step");
  - \b Delete removes the selected values;
  - \b Copy copies the selected values (or all of them) to the clipboard, and \b Paste adds numbers copied from another
    axis, from a text file or from a spreadsheet. Ctrl+C and Ctrl+V also work on the list;
  - to select several values, hold Ctrl or Shift while clicking.
- <b>Spacing</b>: the cell size varies along the axis according to a function that you write, as in the Body Fitting
  algorithm of SMESH. Along the axis, a parameter t goes from 0 (start of the bounding box) to 1 (end of the bounding
  box), and the function f(t) gives the cell size at that position.

  You can also split the axis into several ranges by giving "internal points" between 0 and 1, with one function per
  range. Within each range, t again goes from 0 to 1. In the dialog, the table lists the ranges with their columns
  <i>From t</i>, <i>To t</i> and <i>f(t)</i>: \b Insert splits the selected range into two, and \b Delete merges it
  with its neighbour. A single range can hold at most about 1000 cells: for a finer grid, add internal points.

<h2>Cells outside all solids</h2>

This option decides what happens to the cells whose center is in no solid:
- \b Remove (the default): these cells are not created.
- \b Keep: these cells are created too, and attached to the main shape instead of a solid. This requires the main shape
  to be a compound (a shape that groups several shapes). A warning gives the number of cells kept this way.

<h2>Getting one mesh group per material</h2>

In the geometry, create one group of solids per material. If your geometry comes from a GDML file imported in SHAPER,
the command <i>Create material groups</i> creates these groups for you, named <code>MAT_&lt;material&gt;</code>.
Then, in the <b>Create Mesh</b> dialog, check <b>Create all groups on geometry</b>: after the compute, the mesh has
one group of hexahedra per material.

The same groups are used to give each cell its material in the
\ref cartesiangridplugin_export_page "Cartesian MED export".

*/
